<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Poyang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Poyang Lake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Poyang is the largest freshwater lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> province, fed by the Gan and other rivers and draining into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. Its area varies greatly between the summer flood season and the dry winter months. In 1363 it was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-poyang">Battle of Lake Poyang</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, the future Hongwu Emperor, destroyed the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a> of Chen Han.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-poyang.jpg" alt="Lake Poyang" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satelites_image_of_Lake_Poyang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emerging from the Red Turban Rebellion (c. 1351–1368), Zhu took Nanjing in 1356 and unified southern China by defeating his rivals, above all Chen Youliang at the great naval battle of <b>Lake Poyang</b> in 1363 and Zhang Shicheng in 1367.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…this contest a single victor emerged: Zhu Yuanzhang, a former Buddhist monk of peasant origin, whose military and administrative ability enabled him to defeat his rivals Chen Youliang, at the great naval battle of <b>Lake Poyang</b> in 1363, and Zhang Shicheng, before proclaiming the Ming Dynasty in 1368.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The fleets of Zhu Yuanzhang and Chen Youliang clashed on <b>Lake Poyang</b> in one of the largest naval battles in history.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On <b>Lake Poyang</b>, the smaller fleet of Zhu Yuanzhang destroyed the tower ships of his rival Chen Youliang with fire ships, and Chen was killed by an arrow.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1363 Chen besieged Nanchang and then met Zhu Yuanzhang on <b>Lake Poyang</b>; after weeks of fighting, including fire-ships, Chen was killed by an arrow.</blockquote><a href="/state/chen-han">Chen Han</a></li></ul></section>
